A recommendation is a category-B-recommendation and is an evidence-type-4-recommendation.
If a clinician changes an opioid-dosage for a patient that receives an opioid-therapy then the clinician should weigh a benefit against a risk and should heed a caution.
If a benefit outweighs a risk then every clinician should work with a patient and should optimize a nonopioid-therapy and should continue an opioid-therapy.
If a benefit does not outweigh a risk then every clinician should optimize a different-therapy and should work with a patient and should use a gradual-taper and should discontinue an opioid.
Every clinician that does not provably detect a life-threatening-warning should avoid an abrupt-discontinuation and should avoid a rapid-dosage-reduction from a higher-dosage.
